 "signs": [
  {
   "text": "KELVINGROVE. Red sandstone, built for the 1901 Exhibition. It has a Spitfire hanging from the ceiling, an elephant called Sophie, a Dalí, and an organ that plays most days. Entry is free. Ask for the elephant.",
   "x": 11.0,
   "y": 13.0
  },
  {
   "text": "GEORGE SQUARE. The front lawn of the City Chambers, lined with statues, with Walter Scott up on a very tall column. It has hosted Christmas markets, film crews and one truly ambitious pigeon.",
   "x": 36.0,
   "y": 18.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE DUKE AND HIS CONE. The Duke of Wellington statue nearly always wears a traffic cone. The cone has been removed many times. Glasgow has put it back many times more.",
   "x": 49.0,
   "y": 22.0
  },
  {
   "text": "SQUINTY BRIDGE. Officially the Clyde Arc, opened in 2006. The arch leans to one side and the deck curves, so everyone calls it the Squinty Bridge. Nobody has ever called it the Clyde Arc without a wee smile.",
   "x": 21.0,
   "y": 35.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE CLYDE. The river that built the city. Once the shipyards on its banks launched a big share of the world's ships. Today its quays are for walking, cycling and watching the water change colour.",
   "x": 40.0,
   "y": 35.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE FINNIESTON CRANE. A giant crane from 1931, built to lift locomotives onto ships bound all over the world. It's a monument now, and still looks like it could lift your house.",
   "x": 51.0,
   "y": 35.0
  },
  {
